#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
# /// script
# dependencies = ["openpyxl"]
# ///
#
# student-record — look up a student, or a whole class, across the course's records
# Usage: student-record [QUERY]             search interactively; Enter copies email
#        student-record --show QUERY        print every matching record and stop
#        student-record --class [SECTION]   how the class, or one section, is doing
#        student-record --emails SECTION    copy the emails of everyone in a section
#        student-record --check             what was read, and every warning
#        student-record --d2l-import        write the file that updates D2L's grade so far
#
#   --as-of YYYY-MM-DD  calculate as if it were this date (default: today)
#   --roster-dir DIR    the class lists (default: the Team's exported-classlists)
#   --sac-workbook F    the SAC list (default: the Team's sac-students.xlsx)
#   --grades-dir DIR    the D2L exports (default: the Team's grades-exported-from-d2l)
#   --assessments F     (default: course-info/assessments.csv)
#   --scheme F          (default: course-info/grading-scheme.csv)
#   --staff F           (default: the Team's TAs-and-other-staff.csv)
#
# In the interactive search, Tab marks several students and Enter copies all of
# their emails, separated by semicolons, ready for Outlook's To line.
#
# IT WRITES NOTHING, except the one file --d2l-import is asked for, which it puts
# beside the D2L exports. Every file is read fresh on each run and there is no cache,
# because a cache would be one more copy of names and grades on disk. The preview
# text travels inside fzf's input for the same reason.
#
# THE JOIN IS ON STUDENT ID. A student comes from up to three places:
#
#   class lists   name, section, whether they dropped (newest list per instructor)
#   D2L export    grades, and an email if the class list has none
#   sac-students  whether they are on the list, and writes-at-sac
#
# A SAC row with no ID yet falls back to matching its name, and says so. Someone
# found in only one place is shown anyway and labelled, because that is usually
# the thing worth knowing about them.
#
# WHAT A RECORD CAN'T TELL YOU, and so always shows alongside:
#
#   how old each source is      "not dropped" only means "not as of that export"
#   instructors with no list    their students are absent, not out of the course
#   "not on the SAC list"       the list is hand-kept, so this is not "not registered"
#
# The marks follow gradebook.py, whose header gives the rules.

